### What is a Semaphore?

A Semaphore is a concurrency utility that controls access to a shared resource by maintaining a count of available permits. It is commonly used to limit the number of threads that can access a resource simultaneously, such as a database connection pool or an API rate limiter.

```java
package com.bank.concurrency;

import java.math.BigDecimal;
import java.util.concurrent.Semaphore;

public class ConnectionLimiterDemo {
    private static final Semaphore dbPool = new Semaphore(5);  // max 5 connections

    static BigDecimal queryBalance(String accountId) throws InterruptedException {
        dbPool.acquire();  // take a permit (blocks if all 5 in use)
        try {
            System.out.println(Thread.currentThread().getName()
                    + " querying " + accountId);
            Thread.sleep(100);  // simulate DB query
            return new BigDecimal("75000.00");
        } finally {
            dbPool.release();  // always release in finally!
        }
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        for (int i = 0; i < 20; i++) {
            final int id = i;
            new Thread(() -> {
                try { queryBalance("SAV" + id); }
                catch (InterruptedException e) { Thread.currentThread().interrupt(); }
            }, "Worker-" + i).start();
        }
        // 20 threads, but max 5 query DB concurrently
    }
}
```

### How is tryAcquire used for non-blocking patterns?

```java
package com.bank.concurrency;

import java.util.concurrent.Semaphore;
import java.util.concurrent.TimeUnit;

public class NonBlockingDemo {
    private static final Semaphore pool = new Semaphore(5);

    static String handleRequest(String accountId) throws Exception {
        // Non-blocking: return immediately if no permit
        if (pool.tryAcquire()) {
            try { return queryDB(accountId); }
            finally { pool.release(); }
        } else {
            return "Service busy, try later";  // fail fast
        }
    }

    static String handleRequestWithTimeout(String accountId) throws Exception {
        // Timeout: wait up to 5 seconds for a permit
        if (pool.tryAcquire(5, TimeUnit.SECONDS)) {
            try { return queryDB(accountId); }
            finally { pool.release(); }
        } else {
            throw new RuntimeException("DB pool full for 5 seconds");
        }
    }

    static String queryDB(String id) { return "balance for " + id; }
}
```

### How is binary semaphore different from synchronized?

| Feature | synchronized / ReentrantLock | Binary Semaphore(1) |
|---|---|---|
| Thread ownership | Yes (only owner unlocks) | No (any thread can release) |
| Reentrant | Yes (same thread re-locks) | No (re-acquire deadlocks) |
| IllegalMonitorStateException | Yes (wrong thread unlocks) | No (any release is valid) |
| Use case | Mutual exclusion | Resource limiting, signaling |

### What are common mistakes?

Forgetting to release in a finally block causes permit leaks, where permits are permanently consumed and eventually all threads block.

```java
// WRONG: no finally – permit leaked on exception
pool.acquire();
riskyOperation();  // throws exception!
pool.release();    // never reached!

// CORRECT: release in finally
pool.acquire();
try {
    riskyOperation();
} finally {
    pool.release();  // always released
}
```

### What are common interview follow-up questions?

- Can you create a semaphore with zero permits? Yes. Useful for signaling: one thread waits on acquire(), another thread calls release() to signal.
- Is Semaphore fair by default? No. The default is non-fair (barging allowed). Pass true to the constructor for FIFO ordering.
